.TH PLOT 5
.SH NAME
plot \- graphics interface
.SH DESCRIPTION
Files of this format are produced by routines described in
.BR plot (3g),
and are interpreted for various devices by
.BR plot (1g).
A graphics file is a stream of plotting instructions. Each instruction consists of an ASCII letter usually followed by bytes of binary information. The instructions are executed in order. A point is designated by four bytes representing the x and y values; each value is a signed integer.
.PP
There are no instructions for openpl() or closepl(), as they are automatically invoked by the filter driver when the
.BR plot (1g)
command is given.
.PP
Each of the following descriptions begins with the name of the corresponding routine in
.BR plot (3g).
See
.BR plot (3g)
for a more detailed description of these routines.
.PP
Standard Unix routines
.TP 4
.B e
erase: Erase the graphics screen. No bytes follow.
.TP
.B s
space: The next two coordinate pairs (four bytes each) give the lower-left and upper-right corners of the user-coordinate system to be defined.
.TP
.B t
label: Place the following ASCII string, ending with a newline, so that its first character falls on the current point.
.TP
.B l
line: The next two coordinate pairs (four bytes each) specify the start and end points of the line to be drawn.
.TP
.B c
circle: The next coordinate pair (four bytes) specify the center and the following two bytes specify the radius of a complete circle.
.TP
.B a
arc: The next three coordinate pairs (four bytes each) give the center point, and starting and ending octants of a counter-clockwise circular arc.
.TP
.B m
move: The next four bytes give a new current point.
.TP
.B n
cont: Draw a line from the old current point to the new current point given by the next four bytes.
.TP
.B p
point: Plot a point at the new current point, given by the next four bytes.
.TP
.B f
linemod: The following ASCII string, ending with a newline, chooses one of the line-styles available in the graphics filter.
.PP
Special VENIX enhancements
.TP
.B g
linepat: The next two bytes specify a 16-bit integer pattern, of the user's choice, to be used as a special line pattern.
.TP
.B h
linewid: The next two bytes set desired width, in user coordinates, of all lines following.
.TP
.B w
window: The next two coordinate pairs (four bytes each) specify bottom-left and upper-right corners of a clipping window in user coordinates.
.TP
.B b
box: The next two coordinate pairs (four bytes each) specify the bottom-left and upper-right corners of a rectangular box.
.TP
.B r
rfill: The next two coordinate pairs (four bytes each) specify the lower-left and upper-right corners of a filled rectangular box.
.TP
.B u
fill: The next four bytes specify the coordinates of a seed point for a general convex boundary fill.
.TP
.B d
dot: The next coordinate pair (four bytes) specify the center location and the following two bytes specify the radius of a filled circle.
.TP
.B o
color: The next two bytes specify a choice from the color palette. All graphics following will be drawn in the specified color.
.TP
.B i
colndx: The first two bytes specify the palette color to be modified, and the next two bytes specify the new color.
.TP
.B j
writemod: The following ASCII string, ending with a newline, chooses one of the writing modes available in the graphics filter.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
plot(1g), plot(3g)
.sp
